Ported fast kernels (owner authorized)
--------------------------------------

The repository owner authorized mapping and copying InSARdev algorithm
source into this MIT tree after speaking with the InSARdev author, Alexey
Pechnikov. The fast CPU path does not import ``insardev`` and does not
need ``pip install insardev``.

Adapted into ``src/ai_insar/pipeline/filtering.py`` from InSARdev
``utils_goldstein.py`` (copyright (c) 2025 Alexey Pechnikov,
https://github.com/InSARdev/core):

- ``goldstein_numpy``: batched ``scipy.fft`` of interferogram patches,
  weight ``|FFT| ** alpha``, triangular overlap-add.
- ``goldstein_pytorch``: the same filter with the FFT on PyTorch when
  ``AI_INSAR_FILTER=torch``. Torch is optional. Tests and the default
  path run with NumPy/SciPy only.

The in-tree filter keeps this project's function arguments (``alpha``,
``window``, ``overlap``, ``spectrum_smooth``). ``alpha`` is the value from
the Goldstein menu, not a per-patch coherence. A small floor on the
triangular window keeps border pixels finite. Spectral smoothing is the
existing ``spectrum_smooth`` argument, applied to ``|FFT|`` before the
power. Overlap-add uses Numba when ``pip install "ai-insar[fast]"`` is
installed, and a Python loop otherwise.

The rest of the InSARdev-SAL-1.0 package (Stack, Batch, the DCT/IRLS
unwrap, and the other ``utils_*`` modules) is not copied. Quality-guided
unwrap stays the in-tree Numba walk. The default unwrap is the separable
path below, which is faster on CPU for smooth phase and does not need
that DCT solver.

InSARdev/edu (MIT)
------------------

Copyright (c) 2026 InSARdev
https://github.com/InSARdev/edu

The MIT license requires this copyright and permission notice to travel
with substantial portions of that software. The educational scripts are
not pasted in as project files. These patterns are reimplemented:

- Fast unwrap in `src/ai_insar/pipeline/unwrap.py` follows
  `NISAR/nisar_numpy.py`: Gaussian smooth, then `numpy.unwrap` along range
  and azimuth, splitting azimuth at the middle of the image.
- Interferograms in `src/ai_insar/pipeline/interferogram.py` are the complex
  product `later * conjugate(earlier)`, the same product as
  `NISAR/nisar_numpy.py` (`ref * conj(rep)` with the later date as ref) and
  `Sentinel-1/s1.py` (`rep_aligned * conj(slc_ref)`).

`Sentinel-1/s1.py` also demonstrates TOPS deramp, OpenCV amplitude
coregistration, OR-Tools branch cuts, and geocoding. Those steps need
orbits, OpenCV, and OR-Tools. They are not included. The SAFE zip stack
uses a teaching crop window instead of that coregistration.

